---
import Article from '../../layouts/Article.astro';
import Answer from '../../components/Answer.astro';
import Claim from '../../components/Claim.astro';
import Disclosure from '../../components/Disclosure.astro';
import CaptureThumb from '../../components/CaptureThumb.astro';
---

<Article
  title="Public statements and actions"
  description="Statements published by Coinkite, custody providers, wallet vendors and community organisations during the COLDCARD entropy incident."
  standfirst="What organisations and individuals said they were doing after disclosure. The record keeps product guidance, operational claims, commercial positions and community actions attributed to the people who published them."
  depth="plain"
  kicker="Responses"
  next={{
    href: '/response/migration/',
    label: 'Migration guidance',
    note: 'The recommendations, assumptions and reported outcomes kept separate from this archive\'s own voice.',
  }}
>
  <Answer>
    <p>
      Custody providers published key-rotation and threshold-policy guidance on
      31 July. Competing wallet vendors published their own scope and scam
      warnings. On 2 August Coinkite reported halting shipments, destroying
      affected stock, contacting customers and supporting migration, then
      acknowledged lasting damage in a Sunday update. Community participants
      also described outreach and organisational changes. Unless the underlying
      action is independently visible, these are records of what each publisher
      said, not confirmation that every described action occurred.
      <Claim
        basis="reported"
        scope="The dated overview of provider, vendor and community statements on this page"
        source="Primary posts and publications preserved in the linked source records"
        href="/record/timeline/"
        compact
      />
    </p>
  </Answer>

  <Disclosure
    id="providers"
    title="Custody providers and wallet vendors"
    note="Guidance, product-scope claims, and the interests behind them"
    open
  >
    <p>
      These statements answer different questions. Unchained and Casa described
      changes to customer policies. Ledger described its own implementation and
      said it was not affected. None is treated as a neutral comparison of
      custody products.
    </p>

    <ol class="statement-list">
      <li>
        <p class="statement-list__meta">31 July 2026 · Unchained</p>
        <h3><a href="/record/sources/unchained-guidance/">Rotate every COLDCARD-generated key</a></h3>
        <p>
          Unchained recommended replacing every COLDCARD-generated key with one
          generated on another device. It described two COLDCARD-generated keys
          in its 2-of-3 vault as the most severe case, while saying one affected
          key is normally below threshold.
          <Claim
            basis="reported"
            captured="1 Aug 2026"
            scope="Unchained's incident guidance and its assessment of 2-of-3 vault exposure"
            source="Unchained's captured public guidance"
            href="/record/sources/unchained-guidance/"
            compact
          />
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p class="statement-list__meta">31 July 2026 · Casa and Nick Neuman</p>
        <h3><a href="/record/sources/casa-incident-guidance/">Keep one manufacturer's failure below threshold</a></h3>
        <p>
          Casa presented multi-key, multi-vendor custody as a way to keep a
          single manufacturer's failure below a spending threshold. Nick Neuman
          also published a migration video using Casa. Both have a commercial
          interest in the service being recommended, and the result depends on
          the policy and origin of every key in it.
          <Claim
            basis="reported"
            captured="1 Aug 2026"
            scope="Casa's multi-vendor-custody position and Neuman's migration and risk claim"
            source="Casa and Nick Neuman, captured primary posts; commercial interest stated above"
            href="/record/sources/nneuman-casa-migration-video/"
            compact
          />
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p class="statement-list__meta">31 July 2026 · Ledger</p>
        <h3><a href="/record/sources/ledger-not-affected-response/">A competing vendor says its devices are not affected</a></h3>
        <p>
          Ledger attributed its not-affected claim to a true-random-number
          generator inside its Secure Element and said each 24-word recovery
          phrase receives 256 bits of entropy. This archive holds the statement
          but has not independently reviewed that implementation. Ledger is a
          competing hardware-wallet vendor.
          <Claim
            basis="reported"
            captured="1 Aug 2026"
            scope="Ledger's not-affected statement and its description of its entropy architecture"
            source="Ledger's captured primary post; implementation not independently assessed here"
            href="/record/sources/ledger-not-affected-response/"
            compact
          />
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>

    <p class="statement-note">
      The threshold mechanics behind the provider statements live on
      <a href="/how-it-broke/conditions/#multisig">the technical
      conditions page</a>. The publishers' migration recommendations and their
      limits live in <a href="/response/migration/">the migration record</a>.
    </p>
  </Disclosure>

  <Disclosure
    id="coinkite"
    title="Coinkite's operational statements"
    note="Shipments, stock, customer contact, support and the Sunday update"
  >
    <p>
      The statements below are the vendor's own accounts of operations that are
      not observable from this archive. They are preserved as published and
      linked to the captured posts.
    </p>

    <ol class="statement-list">
      <li>
        <p class="statement-list__meta">2 August 2026, 18:04 UTC · Coinkite</p>
        <h3><a href="/record/sources/coldcard-shipments-halted/">Shipments halted and remaining affected stock destroyed</a></h3>
        <p>
          Coinkite said it halted shipments once the vulnerability was
          confirmed, destroyed remaining units carrying affected firmware and
          emailed customers whose devices had shipped. The post also said
          SATSCARD, OPENDIME and TAPSIGNER use different codebases and are not
          affected.
          <Claim
            basis="reported"
            scope="The vendor's shipment, stock, customer-email and product-scope statements"
            source="Coinkite's captured official post of 2 Aug 2026"
            href="/record/sources/coldcard-shipments-halted/"
            compact
          />
        </p>
        <CaptureThumb id="coldcard-shipments-halted" />
      </li>

      <li>
        <p class="statement-list__meta">2 August 2026, 23:00 UTC · Coinkite</p>
        <h3><a href="/record/sources/coldcard-update-sunday/">The Sunday update acknowledges lasting damage</a></h3>
        <p>
          The vendor described customer outreach and migration help, thanked
          community members who helped through the weekend and said the damage
          done is permanent, with hard questions remaining about the company.
          <Claim
            basis="reported"
            scope="The Sunday statement's damage acknowledgement, outreach description, community thanks and forward commitment"
            source="Coinkite's captured official post of 2 Aug 2026"
            href="/record/sources/coldcard-update-sunday/"
            compact
          />
        </p>
        <CaptureThumb id="coldcard-update-sunday" />
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h3 id="outreach">The outreach and data-retention exchange</h3>

    <p>
      Coinkite said phone numbers and customer personal data are deleted, that
      many customers were therefore unreachable by email and that an earlier
      post could have been worded better.
      <Claim
        basis="reported"
        scope="Coinkite's statement on outreach data handling and deletion"
        source="Coinkite's captured official reply of 2 Aug 2026"
        href="/record/sources/coldcard-pii-policy/"
        compact
      />
      Satochip then asked how store customers received emails if Coinkite
      deletes data after 90 days. The 90-day period is Satochip's premise, not
      an established fact, and Satochip is a competing hardware-wallet vendor.
      <Claim
        basis="reported"
        scope="Satochip's public data-retention question and its 90-day premise"
        source="Satochip's captured post of 2 Aug 2026; competing interest stated above"
        href="/record/sources/satochip-data-retention-question/"
        compact
      />
      Coinkite's separate June paper-spam statement gives 120 days for deletion
      of customer data including physical addresses. No held source reconciles
      the exchange.
    </p>
  </Disclosure>

  <Disclosure
    id="positions"
    title="Wider public positions"
    note="Arguments prompted by the incident, not findings the incident can settle"
  >
    <p>
      NCFA Canada argued that self custody removes the custodian but not the
      vendor layer beneath it: the device, its firmware, seed-generation code
      and update path. This defect did sit in that layer. Its comparison with
      regulated custody is an argument about where risk is placed, not something
      this incident measures, and its loss figures are carried secondhand from
      Galaxy Research. NCFA Canada is a fintech industry association whose
      members include financial-technology and digital-asset businesses.
      <Claim
        basis="reported"
        captured="5 Aug 2026"
        scope="NCFA Canada's vendor-layer position and its secondhand use of Galaxy Research's loss figures"
        source="NCFA Canada's captured 3 August commentary; affiliation stated above"
        href="/record/sources/ncfacanada-self-custody-commentary/"
        compact
      />
    </p>

    <p class="statement-note">
      Other broad positions are kept beside the evidence needed to read them:
      the <a href="/response/ai/">open-source and AI discovery dispute</a>,
      <a href="/how-it-broke/conditions/#dice">dice claims</a>, and the
      <a href="/response/migration/#threshold-wallets">first-spend debate</a>.
      This avoids maintaining a second verdict table here.
    </p>
  </Disclosure>

  <Disclosure
    id="community"
    title="Community and organisational actions"
    note="Outreach, support and a board departure published during the incident weekend"
  >
    <ol class="statement-list">
      <li>
        <p class="statement-list__meta">2 August 2026 · Marty Bent</p>
        <h3><a href="/record/sources/martybent-endorsement-apology/">An endorsement apology and an account of migration help</a></h3>
        <p>
          Bent apologised to people who bought a COLDCARD on his recommendation
          and described spending the weekend helping people move funds.
          <Claim
            basis="reported"
            scope="Bent's apology and his account of helping people migrate"
            source="Marty Bent's captured post of 2 Aug 2026"
            href="/record/sources/martybent-endorsement-apology/"
            compact
          />
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p class="statement-list__meta">2 August 2026 · Pledditor</p>
        <h3><a href="/record/sources/pledditor-owner-outreach/">A community member describes direct owner outreach</a></h3>
        <p>
          One individual described finding older posts that mentioned buying a
          COLDCARD and replying with an alert about the incident, while accepting
          the risk of account moderation.
          <Claim
            basis="reported"
            scope="The described owner-outreach effort and its accepted moderation risk"
            source="Pledditor's captured post of 2 Aug 2026"
            href="/record/sources/pledditor-owner-outreach/"
            compact
          />
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p class="statement-list__meta">2 August 2026, 20:44 UTC · OpenSats</p>
        <h3><a href="/record/sources/opensats-nvk-board-departure/">NVK steps down from the OpenSats board</a></h3>
        <p>
          OpenSats announced the departure as immediately effective and said
          the board would continue with eight members. The post gave no reason.
          Its timing falls within the incident weekend, but the source itself
          does not connect the two.
          <Claim
            basis="reported"
            scope="The OpenSats board departure and interim board size; no causal link to the incident"
            source="OpenSats' captured statement of 2 Aug 2026"
            href="/record/sources/opensats-nvk-board-departure/"
            compact
          />
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </Disclosure>
</Article>

<style>
  .statement-list {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 1rem 0 0;
    padding: 0;
  }
  .statement-list > li {
    padding: 1rem 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }
  .statement-list > li:last-child { border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule); }
  .statement-list h3 { margin-top: .3rem; font-size: 1rem; }
  .statement-list h3 + p { margin-top: .45rem; }
  .statement-list__meta {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: .64rem;
    letter-spacing: .07em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--ink-3);
  }
  .statement-note {
    margin-top: 1rem;
    padding: .8rem .9rem;
    border-left: 2px solid var(--rule-2);
    background: var(--surface-2);
    color: var(--ink-2);
    font-size: .88rem;
  }
</style>
